Small Talk PS
A notification popped up on my iPad, blocking the brush selection tool of the digital painting I was working on. It was Jacob, sending a reel featuring a philosophical quote. Jacob was traditionally reserved, quiet, and stubbornly by-the-book—the polar opposite of my instinct to constantly challenge instructions and to optimize every system around me. But on this afternoon particular afternoon, we began debating the video as our conversation drifted from philosophy to our own social lives. 
I readily explained my deep dissatisfaction with the standard high school social scene—particularly about the way people tend to socialize. But what I really detailed is my hearty disdain for small talk. I told him that I often preselect my social circle by hypothesizing their underlying personal life principles based on their daily actions and demeanor before speaking much to them. To me, there was nothing meaningful to learn when conversing about insignificant events or gossip. Focus ends up being redirected to those trivialities rather than directly connecting to the person being spoken to, and ultimately just leads to an illusion of companionship that is weak and can break as time goes on and principles reveal themselves. 
Mid-explanation, a text bubble caught me entirely off guard: 
"I disagree but continue." 
It had been a long time since anyone had actively challenged my self-proclaimed wisdom instead of just politely listening. Determined to defend my logic, I pushed back, but Jacob quietly dismantled my framework. He pointed out that by always trying to achieve that specific kind of exhausting, intense, and existential conversation, I was treating people as entertainment or data rather than appreciating their inherent value. Small talk, he argued, wasn’t vain; it was a mechanism to build fundamental trust, ensuring people feel heard for what they experience rather than evaluated for how they think and what they believe. 
This wasn't the first time Jacob quietly grounded me. It’s hilariously ironic that the most reserved guy in my circle is the one pushing me toward actual emotional intelligence. But it makes sense—my mind is always racing to find a formula, whereas Jacob has the patience to just sit back and watch. Although both of us are observant in contrasting ways, he emphasized to me that sometimes slower judgement is a superpower, and that my optimizing mentality actually hinders building a desirable sense of companionship. 
The next day at lunch, I sat down next to a classmate I had shared a table with for months but to whom I’d never truly spoken. Swallowing my pride, I forced my analytical urge down to take a back seat and asked one of Jacob's suggested icebreakers: 
“Soooo, what’s your favorite time of the day?” 
